  "findings": "Measured Application-side A_ activities, Workflow W_ activities, and compared them to Offer-side O_ activities using median sojourn and waiting times. Using a simple additive decomposition of activity-level medians, non-offer components are substantial but generally smaller than offer-side components. For sojourn time, Offer-side O_ activities sum to 1,727,632.0 seconds in medians, while non-offer A_ + W_ activities sum to 1,105,584.5 seconds; the non-offer share is approximately 39.0%, and the offer-side share is approximately 61.0%. For waiting time, Offer-side O_ activities sum to 4,020,604.5 seconds in medians, while non-offer A_ + W_ activities sum to 3,551,390.5 seconds; the non-offer share is approximately 46.9%, and the offer-side share is approximately 53.1%. Combining sojourn and waiting medians, Offer-side activities contribute 5,748,236.5 seconds versus 4,656,975.0 seconds for non-offer activities, i.e. roughly 55.2% offer-side and 44.8% non-offer. Workflow W_ activities have very small median sojourn and waiting times compared with both A_ and O_ components, despite high event counts. Non-offer delay is dominated by Application-level waiting at A_Cancelled and A_Validating and sojourn at A_Complete, A_Incomplete, A_Concept, and A_Validating. Offer-side delay is dominated by sojourn at O_Sent (mail and online), O_Sent (online only), and O_Returned, and by waiting at O_Cancelled, O_Returned, O_Refused, and O_Accepted. Therefore, reducing offer processing time would meaningfully shift overall flow time only under the assumption that the large O_ sojourn/waiting intervals are controllable operational processing delays rather than customer response time, terminal cancellation latency, or external waiting. If only the short technical creation steps O_Create Offer and O_Created are reducible, the impact would be negligible. If the large O_Sent/O_Returned/O_Cancelled/O_Accepted/O_Refused intervals can be reduced, offer-side reductions could affect a majority-like share of the measured activity-level median time, but non-offer A_ delays remain large enough that they would limit the overall improvement.",
